CZ makes a great point: accessing a website can be exploited.

 In this context, the "verifiable frontend" solution—introduced by Blon via "Deweb" (built on Tapeout)—works as follows: HashPort stores a dApp’s static frontend (HTML, JS, CSS, WASM, etc.) directly on the BNB Chain: Each file’s SHA-256 hash is calculated and registered on-chain. The wallet or browser performs a byte-level hash comparison of the loaded files to ensure they match the on-chain version (verifying it as "Published"). Smart contracts can declare their official frontend within a public, decentralized registry. If the page content does not match the registered version, the wallet issues a "Wrong frontend" warning (establishing an "Official" status). Frontend ownership is managed via a "Circuit Container" NFT, eliminating reliance on traditional account-password credentials. This approach addresses common security threats in the crypto space, such as frontend hijacking, CDN/DNS tampering, and phishing sites masquerading as legitimate interfaces.

@Blonskr @TapeOutWorld @Hashport01

